$DIR/main.p:25:10: s holds a view of a (a.Slice(), line 22), which was passed to grow(b), which may free what it looks at or move it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434) at line 24, so s can no longer be used here
    s looks at memory that a refers to, without owning it: the view was made by
    a.Slice() at line 22. A view obtained through a counted reference held in a
    local variable may be used only while the variable keeps the reference the
    view was made from. A call that may free memory the view looks at, or move
    an owner out of it, through a pointer to the variable or to memory it owns
    ends the view as well. At line 24 a was passed to grow(b), which may free
    what it looks at or move it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0],
    len(old)), vec.p:434), so the view may look at memory that was released, or
    at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use s before line 24, or make that call only after the last use of s
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
